// `internal` on the interaction layer (typl §3.3, ADR-0008 decision 7).
//
// `internal` is accepted on an `interface` — not on an interaction and not on a
// service, which are parse errors — and the IR records it faithfully
// (`Interface.visibility = 2`). What the backends do with it is the point of
// this file: it is what caught them dropping `internal` entirely. See
// `veh-cluster/NOTES`, "`internal` was dropped by both backends".
//
// It is also the corpus's **legal** case for the visibility rule TYPL-005 states
// (issue #161): `WheelDiagnostics` is package-private and names a
// package-private payload in every position, which must keep compiling. The
// checker leaves it alone and the generated Rust is `pub(crate)` on both sides,
// so `veh_cluster_generated_rust_compiles_with_rustc` — which denies
// `private_interfaces` — is the proof rather than a snapshot comparison.
//
// A **tuple** under an `internal` declaration is here for the same reason
// (issue #167). Until that fix the file deliberately held none: a tuple
// generates a named struct of its own, that struct was emitted `pub` whatever
// induced it, and the compile proof would have failed on a program the checker
// correctly accepts. So "an `internal` interface over an `internal` payload
// compiles" was proven for the tuple-free subset only, while
// `internal_interface_may_name_internal_types_in_every_position` in `ridl-sem`
// asserted the tuple position legal. Both halves now say the same thing.

package veh.cluster

/// A package-private payload type, so the interaction layer's legal
/// `internal`-over-`internal` case is exercised inside one package. The
/// cross-package counterpart is impossible by construction: a foreign
/// `internal` name never resolves (typl §3.3).
internal type RawTickCount : integer [0..65535]

// A package-private struct holding a tuple, so the **typl** half of issue #167
// is in the corpus too: the struct `span` induces is named after `RawWheelSpan`
// and belongs to it, so it is package-private with it.
//
// `bursts` is the second half, and it is here for issue #177: an array **whose
// element is a tuple**. No corpus entry held that combination — `veh-common`
// has an array of a named type (`readings : [Speed; 8]`) and a bare tuple field
// (`range : (min, max)`) but never the two nested — so no generated init ever
// put an object literal in a concise arrow body, and the TypeScript backend
// emitted `() => { first: … }`, which parses as a block and returns
// `undefined`. `span` beside it is the control: a tuple in direct field
// position was correct throughout, and the two now sit in one generated init
// function, so the snapshot shows both forms next to each other.

/// The window of raw tick counts a wheel diagnostic covers.
internal struct RawWheelSpan {
  span   : (first : RawTickCount, latest : RawTickCount)
  bursts : [(first : RawTickCount, latest : RawTickCount); 2]
}

/// A package-private contract shape: realized inside this package, never part
/// of the cross-package surface.
internal interface WheelDiagnostics {
  signal wheelTicks : RawTickCount @[100ms..1s]
  event slipDetected : FilterState @[50ms..500ms]
  query readTicks(): RawTickCount
  // Appended, so the ordinals above do not move (ridl §11). A parameter is its
  // own exposure position: it is one of the positions rustc reported when a
  // public interface carried an `internal` payload.
  command resetTicks(seed: RawTickCount)
  // The tuple return — one of the two interaction positions a tuple can reach.
  // A signal or event payload and a parameter are narrowed to a named type
  // (FORM-102), and a collection return is FORM-101.
  query readSpan(): (min : RawTickCount, max : RawTickCount)
  // The other one, and the position that had no corpus entry at all: FORM-102
  // narrows a `fixed` payload to "a named type OR AN ARRAY" and does not
  // descend into the element, so a tuple reaches an interaction through the
  // array and induces `WheelDiagnosticsTickBoundsElement`. Everything
  // `field_type_tokens` can build is reachable below that element.
  fixed tickBounds : [(low : RawTickCount, high : RawTickCount); 2]
}

/// A public shape beside it, so the generated output shows the two side by
/// side and the difference is readable in one snapshot.
interface WheelSummary {
  signal wheelHealth : FanLevel @[1s..10s]
}
